+### Mixed Parallelism for 3DGS
+- **step**: Distribute Gaussian transformation forward computations Gaussian-wise. Distribute Render Backward and Loss Backward computations pixel-wise, similar to Render Forward. Save independent states (transformed states, gradients, optimizer states, parameters) for each Gaussian on the GPU that contains their parameters. Manage activation states (significant Z-buffers, auxiliary buffers for sorting, loss intermediate activations) pixel-wise along with the image distribution. Perform densification (clone, split, prune) locally on the GPU that stores the Gaussians, as it is based on their independent variance [S6].
 
 ## Background
-The acceleration of 3D Gaussian Splatting has become a critical research area following the introduction of the original 3DGS method, which demonstrated high-quality novel view synthesis but suffered from high memory consumption and computational cost during training and rendering. Early acceleration efforts focused on software-level optimizations such as compression and efficient sorting algorithms. As the field expanded to include dynamic scenes, large-scale environments, and mobile deployment, hardware-specific optimizations emerged. The Roofline model has been applied to identify bottlenecks in the 3DGS pipeline, leading to methods like RoofGS that separate memory-bound and compute-bound optimizations [S1, unofficial]. Concurrently, the need for training on consumer hardware led to memory management strategies like CLM, which offload optimizer states and non-critical attributes to the CPU [S1, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. The utilization of modern GPU features, such as Tensor Cores, has also driven the development of GEMM-GS, which reformulates the blending process to leverage matrix multiplication units [S4]. Mobile deployment requirements have further spurred sorting-free methods like Mobile-GS, which trade exact sorting for view-dependent weighting to reduce latency [S3, unofficial].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S3, unofficial]. The initial implementation relied on standard CUDA kernels for rasterization, which became a bottleneck for real-time performance on large scenes. Subsequent research focused on hardware-specific optimizations. Roofline modeling was applied to identify memory-bound stages [S1, unofficial]. Tensor Core utilization was explored through GEMM reformulation [S4]. Memory management strategies like CLM were developed to enable training on consumer hardware [S1, unofficial]. Distributed training frameworks were adapted to handle multi-GPU scaling [S5, unofficial][S6]. Mobile deployment required sorting-free approximations [S3, unofficial].
 
